"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["school_physics_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["school_physics_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["school_physics_en"][1]={"school_physics_en:M1-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M1-L1 – What is physics? Observation, explanation and models<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M1 – Understanding and investigating physics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Lower secondary education – introduction<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> Everyday observations; no physics\nequations are required.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish observation,\nquestion, hypothesis, model and testable prediction in an example.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>A soft ball is released from a small height. It moves towards the\nground. Why? The statement “The ball moves downwards” describes\n<strong>what we observe<\/strong>. “The Earth attracts the ball” attempts\na <strong>physical explanation<\/strong>. The distinction matters:\nexplanations must hold up against observations and experiments.<\/p>\n<p>Physics investigates natural and technological processes. It combines\ncareful observation, measurement, testable ideas and models. A model is\nnot a complete copy of the real world; it is a simplified description\ndesigned to answer a specific question.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Observing, explaining and predicting<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Observation:<\/strong> A released ball speeds up as it falls.\nThis can be checked using a sequence of video frames.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Question:<\/strong> Does the falling time change when the\nrelease height increases?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Hypothesis:<\/strong> Under otherwise identical conditions, a\nball takes longer to fall from a greater height.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Model:<\/strong> A simple model represents the ball as a\npoint acted on by Earth’s gravitational attraction. Air resistance is\nignored at first.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">5.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Prediction:<\/strong> With all other conditions equal, the\ngreater distance should lead to a longer falling time.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">6.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Test:<\/strong> Compare several drops from different heights\nusing a suitable timing method. Measurements may support or contradict\nthe hypothesis.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>Important: One measurement does not conclusively prove a general\nstatement. Measurement errors, uncontrolled influences and limitations\nof the model must be considered.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_001_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Three panels show a falling ball, a simplified gravitational model and a prediction about fall time.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Three panels show a falling ball, a\nsimplified gravitational model and a prediction about fall\ntime.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Observation, model and prediction are different parts of a\nphysics investigation.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example: two release heights<\/h2>\n<p>Keep the ball, surface and measurement method unchanged while\ncomparing release heights of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.4\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.8\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFirst predict: “The fall from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.8\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntakes longer.” Next record several drops on video rather than attempting\nto time such short intervals manually with a stopwatch.<\/p>\n<p><strong>What changes?<\/strong> Release height. <strong>What is\nmeasured?<\/strong> Time until the first contact with the ground.\n<strong>What stays the same?<\/strong> Ball, release without a push and\nmeasurement method. Record the data in a table before deciding whether\nthey support the prediction.<\/p>\n<p>This experiment tests behaviour only in this setup. It does not\nautomatically answer what happens with strong air resistance or on\nanother celestial body.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your understanding<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Classify: A “The ball hits the ground”; B “The Earth exerts an\nattractive force”; C “A greater release height leads to a longer fall.”\nLabel each as an <strong>observation<\/strong>,\n<strong>explanation<\/strong> or <strong>testable\nprediction<\/strong>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A team changes both ball type and release height at the same time.\nWhy does that make their investigation harder?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A model ignores air resistance. Give an example of an object for\nwhich that could be especially problematic.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A is the observation, B the explanation, C the prediction. C must be\ntested.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Two factors change together; a change in fall time cannot clearly be\nattributed to either one.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A flat sheet of paper, for example, is strongly affected by air\nresistance.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limits<\/h2>\n<p>Representing a ball as a point neglects its shape and rotation.\nNeglecting air resistance is only justified within a simplified model.\nLimit drops to small, safe heights above a soft, clear surface; avoid\nheavy or fragile objects. Repeat measurements.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and next steps<\/h2>\n<p>Physics develops knowledge by moving between observations,\nhypotheses, models and tests. Predictions must be formulated so they can\nbe assessed through observation. The next lesson introduces quantities\nand units for reporting measurements unambiguously.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Physical quantities, units and SI\nprefixes<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.kmk.org/fileadmin/Dateien/veroeffentlichungen_beschluesse/2024/2024_06_13-WeBiS_Physik_MSA.pdf\">German\nStanding Conference of Education Ministers (KMK) – Updated educational\nstandards for physics, intermediate school-leaving certificate\n(2024)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – Scientific inquiry section, pp. 9–10, especially E1–E3;\nbasis for hypotheses, model testing and experiments. <strong>Last\nchecked:<\/strong> 2026-11-21.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M1-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M1-L2 – Physical quantities, units and SI prefixes<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M1 – Understanding and investigating physics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Lower secondary education – introduction<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> Observations and measurement\nquestions from <a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L1<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Express measurement values using\na number and an SI unit, use consistent units and convert kilo-, centi-\nand milli- correctly.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>“The table is 120 long” leaves out a crucial detail. Does 120 mean\nmillimetres, centimetres or metres? A complete measurement consists of a\n<strong>numerical value<\/strong> and a <strong>unit<\/strong>:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>l<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>120<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">l=120\\,\\mathrm{cm}=1.20\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nHere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>l<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">l<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrepresents length.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Quantities and units<\/h2>\n<p>A physical <strong>quantity<\/strong> is something measurable, such as\nlength\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>l<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">l<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ntime\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nor mass\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>m<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA quantity value includes both a number and a unit. Common SI base units\ninclude:<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Quantity<\/th>\n<th>Symbol<\/th>\n<th>SI unit<\/th>\n<th>Symbol<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Length<\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>l<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">l<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>metre<\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Time<\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>second<\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mass<\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>m<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>kilogram<\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Temperature<\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>kelvin<\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{K}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p><strong>Note:<\/strong> The SI base unit of mass is the\n<em>kilogram<\/em>, not the gram. The gram is a common submultiple:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1000<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm{kg}=1000\\,\\mathrm{g}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSpeed is a derived quantity measured in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>Prefixes change the scale, not the type of quantity:<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Prefix<\/th>\n<th>Symbol<\/th>\n<th>Factor<\/th>\n<th>Example<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>kilo<\/td>\n<td>k<\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10^{3}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1000<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm{km}=1000\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>centi<\/td>\n<td>c<\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10^{-2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.01<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm{cm}=0.01\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>milli<\/td>\n<td>m<\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10^{-3}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.001<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm{mm}=0.001\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>Prefix and unit symbols are joined without a space: <code>cm<\/code>,\n<code>mm<\/code>, <code>km<\/code>. Leave a space between the number and\nthe unit: <code>15 cm<\/code>. Unit symbols are case-sensitive:\n<code>m<\/code> means metre, while uppercase <code>M<\/code> as a prefix\nmeans mega.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_002_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Ruler from 0 to 20 centimetres highlighting a 15-centimetre length equal to 150 millimetres and 0.15 metres.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Ruler from 0 to 20 centimetres\nhighlighting a 15-centimetre length equal to 150 millimetres and 0.15\nmetres.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>A length of 15 cm can also be written as 150 mm or 0.15\nm.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Length:<\/strong> A distance is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>350<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">350\\,\\mathrm{mm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSince\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.001<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm{mm}=0.001\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>350<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>350<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.001<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.350<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">350\\,\\mathrm{mm}=350\\cdot0.001\\,\\mathrm{m}=0.350\\,\\mathrm{m}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Time:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>60<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>90<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.5\\,\\mathrm{min}=1.5\\cdot60\\,\\mathrm{s}=90\\,\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe minute is not an SI base unit, but it is commonly used.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Area:<\/strong> When squaring a length, square its conversion\nfactor too. Thus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>000<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm{m^2}=(100\\,\\mathrm{cm})^2=10\\,000\\,\\mathrm{cm^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nDo not convert areas using the linear factor alone.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your understanding<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Convert\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2.5\\,\\mathrm{km}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto metres and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>24<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">24\\,\\mathrm{cm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto metres.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">An experiment lasts\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>30<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\,\\mathrm{min}\\,30\\,\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nExpress this time entirely in seconds.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>500<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">500\\,\\mathrm{cm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nequal? Show one conversion step.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm{m^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nnot equal to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">100\\,\\mathrm{cm^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2500<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2.5\\,\\mathrm{km}=2500\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>24<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.24<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">24\\,\\mathrm{cm}=0.24\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>60<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>30<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>150<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\cdot60\\,\\mathrm{s}+30\\,\\mathrm{s}=150\\,\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Yes.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>500<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>500<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.01<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">500\\,\\mathrm{cm}=500\\cdot0.01\\,\\mathrm{m}=5\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Both dimensions are converted when a unit is squared:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>100<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>000<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">100\\cdot100=10\\,000<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limits<\/h2>\n<p>Correct units do not guarantee sufficient measurement accuracy.\nWriting\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.350<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.350\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nimplies a different stated precision from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.35<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.35\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nwhether the extra digit is justified depends on the measuring\ninstrument. Prefixes express powers of ten, not measurement\nuncertainty.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and next steps<\/h2>\n<p>In physics, a measurement without a unit is usually incomplete. Check\nunit consistency before calculating. The next topic is evaluating\nrepeated measurements and their uncertainty.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L3 – Measurements, repeated\nmeasurements and uncertainty<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.kmk.org/fileadmin/Dateien/veroeffentlichungen_beschluesse/2024/2024_06_13-WeBiS_Physik_MSA.pdf\">German\nStanding Conference of Education Ministers (KMK) – Updated educational\nstandards for physics, intermediate school-leaving certificate\n(2024)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – Subject knowledge, communication and scientific inquiry\nsections (pp. 6–11); curriculum context for physical quantities, units,\nmeasurements and data presentation. <strong>Last checked:<\/strong>\n2026-11-21.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M1-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M1-L3 – Measurements, repeated measurements and uncertainty<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M1 – Understanding and investigating physics<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>Repeating a short measurement five times often produces slightly\ndifferent values. Differences may come from the operator, the instrument\nor changing conditions. A series makes the variation visible, but it\ncannot replace a description of the measurement procedure.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Repeated measurements and analysis<\/h2>\n<p>The following are <strong>invented practice values<\/strong>, not\nrecorded experimental data.<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Trial<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">1<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">2<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">3<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">4<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">5<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Time\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin s<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1.58<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1.62<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1.60<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1.59<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1.61<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The arithmetic mean is the sum of the readings divided by their\nnumber:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>t<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1.58<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1.62<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1.60<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1.59<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1.61<\/mn><\/mrow><mn>5<\/mn><\/mfrac><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.60<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar t=\\frac{1.58+1.62+1.60+1.59+1.61}{5}\\,\\mathrm{s}=1.60\\,\\mathrm{s}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The <strong>range<\/strong> is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.62<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1.58<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.04<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.62\\,\\mathrm{s}-1.58\\,\\mathrm{s}=0.04\\,\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIt is the difference between the largest and smallest observed readings\nand <strong>must not be confused with the complete measurement\nuncertainty<\/strong>. An uncertainty assessment also needs the\ninstrument resolution, calibration and other influences.<\/p>\n<p>A random effect may move individual readings in either direction. A\nsystematic influence, such as a stopwatch that always shows\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.10\\,\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntoo much, shifts the whole series together. Taking an average does not\nremove that constant offset. The offset is a thought experiment, not a\ndocumented fault.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_003_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Five readings from 1.58 to 1.62 seconds are plotted on a time scale; a vertical line marks the mean at 1.60 seconds.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Five readings from 1.58 to 1.62 seconds\nare plotted on a time scale; a vertical line marks the mean at 1.60\nseconds.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Invented example data with mean 1.60 s and range 0.04 s.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example<\/h2>\n<p>A team records\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2.0\\,\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2.2\\,\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2.1\\,\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>t<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2.2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2.1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar t=(2.0+2.2+2.1)\\,\\mathrm{s}/3=2.1\\,\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe range is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.2\\,\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThese figures describe the readings; they do not establish accuracy\nrelative to a reference value.<\/p>\n<p>A measurement report needs units, instrument, setup, individual\nreadings, conditions and any irregularities. The final digit displayed\nby a device does not automatically indicate the accuracy of the entire\nexperiment.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your understanding<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find the mean of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>3.1<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3.1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>3.2<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3.3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3.3\\,\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find the range of the five times in S2.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A clock always runs 5% fast. Is this random or systematic?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is half the range not a complete uncertainty assessment?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3.1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>3.2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>3.3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(3.1+3.2+3.3)/3\\,\\mathrm{s}=3.2\\,\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.04<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.04\\,\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Systematic; simply repeating the test will not remove it.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The range does not separately address calibration, resolution or\nother sources of error.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limits<\/h2>\n<p>An average summarises readings taken under comparable conditions. It\nmay be misleading if conditions change during the trials. Statistical\nstandard uncertainty requires a more rigorous method and is a topic for\nlater study.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and next steps<\/h2>\n<p>Repeated readings reveal variation. Comparisons also require\ncontrolled conditions, which are the topic of <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L4<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.kmk.org/fileadmin/Dateien/veroeffentlichungen_beschluesse/2024/2024_06_13-WeBiS_Physik_MSA.pdf\">KMK\n– Educational standards in physics for the intermediate school-leaving\ncertificate (2024)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · pp. 8–11, scientific methods and inquiry · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.bipm.org/documents/20126/2071204/JCGM_100_2008_E.pdf\">JCGM\n– Guide to the Expression of Uncertainty in Measurement (2008)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nsection 4.2, means and variation (advanced reference) · Last accessed:\n2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M1-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M1-L4 – Hypotheses, variables and fair experiments<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M1 – Understanding and investigating physics<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>A small ball rolls down a sloping track. Will it reach the end sooner\nif the track is steeper? We can test the idea, but only when the\nconditions can be compared meaningfully.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Hypothesis and variables<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Question:<\/strong> How does the starting height\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>h<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\naffect the rolling time\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/p>\n<p><strong>Hypothesis:<\/strong> With the same track and ball, a greater\nstarting height reduces the rolling time. This is testable, but not yet\nan observed result.<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Role<\/th>\n<th>Quantity<\/th>\n<th>Procedure<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Independent<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>Starting height\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>h<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>deliberately change it, for example between 10 cm and 20 cm<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Dependent<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>Rolling time\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>measure it on every run<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Controlled<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>Ball, track length, release point, timing method<\/td>\n<td>keep as constant as possible<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>Both heights are example settings for a <strong>planned<\/strong>\nexperiment, not documented results. Use one fixed track mounted securely\nat two different inclines using a low, stable support. Release the ball\nwithout pushing it, always from the same position along the track.\nRecord several trials per setting.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Original teaching graphic:<\/strong> Two schematic tracks of\nequal length distinguish the varied height from controlled quantities.\nThe drawing does not claim experimental results.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_004_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two same-length ball tracks start at different heights, 10 and 20 centimetres; same ball and release position.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two same-length ball tracks start at\ndifferent heights, 10 and 20 centimetres; same ball and release\nposition.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Design: deliberately vary starting height and measure rolling\ntime repeatedly.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example: Improving an ambiguous experiment<\/h2>\n<p>A team first uses a short wooden track and a metal ball, then a\nlonger plastic track and a different ball. They attribute the observed\ntimes to height alone. This is <strong>not a fair comparison<\/strong>:\nlength, track material and ball also changed.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Better plan:<\/strong> Deliberately change only the height.\nKeep the other named factors constant. Tabulate the times and consider\nthe variation described in <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L3<\/a>. The lower starting height\ncan serve as a <strong>reference condition<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your understanding<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The slope of a toy ramp is changed, and the time to reach the bottom\nis measured. Name the independent and dependent quantities.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Name three conditions to keep constant.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A team pushes the ball at the start of its second trial. What is\nwrong with the comparison?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Write a testable prediction for the two starting heights.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Independent: slope or starting height; dependent: rolling time.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">For example the ball, track length, release point and timing\nmethod.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Initial velocity changed as well as height, so the cause of any\ndifference is ambiguous.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">For example: “At the greater starting height the average rolling\ntime is shorter, with other conditions held constant.”<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limits<\/h2>\n<p>A comparison cannot remove every possible disturbing influence. The\ntrack must be set up so that its length and the release point along it\nremain identical in both configurations. Safety comes first: use a\nlightweight ball, a low height, stable supports, a clear catching area\nand teacher instruction. Never build an unsecured structure above people\nor on unstable furniture.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and next steps<\/h2>\n<p>A fair comparison changes as few relevant factors as possible,\nideally only the one being tested. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L5<\/a> shows how to summarise\nresults in tables and graphs.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.kmk.org/fileadmin/Dateien/veroeffentlichungen_beschluesse/2024/2024_06_13-WeBiS_Physik_MSA.pdf\">KMK\n– Educational standards in physics for the intermediate school-leaving\ncertificate (2024)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · pp. 8–11, scientific methods and inquiry · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.iqb.hu-berlin.de/media/documents/KSM_Physik.pdf\">IQB –\nPhysics proficiency-level models<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · pp. 44–48, variable control,\ngraphs and model limitations · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content\nverified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.sichere-schule.de/physik/praktisches-arbeiten/anforderungen-intro\">DGUV\n– Safe practical work in physics<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · safety, experiment instructions\nand apparatus · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M1-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M1-L5 – Tables, graphs and proportionality<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M1 – Understanding and investigating physics<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>A table contains individual pairs of values. A graph helps us see how\none quantity changes with another. We need sensible scales and clear\naxis labels containing both <strong>quantity and unit<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Data tables and proportionality<\/h2>\n<p>These are <strong>invented, ideally proportional model\nvalues<\/strong>, not experimental measurements. In the model, an object\ntravels two metres every second.<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Time\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin s<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">0<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">1<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">2<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">3<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">4<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Distance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>s<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin m<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">2<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">4<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">6<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">8<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The independent quantity\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbelongs on the horizontal x-axis and distance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>s<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\non the vertical y-axis. Each point gives a pair\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(t,s)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor these values, the points lie on a straight line through the\norigin.<\/p>\n<p>Two quantities are <strong>directly proportional<\/strong> if\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s=k\\,t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor a constant factor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwe obtain:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mi>s<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=\\frac{s}{t}=\\frac{6\\,\\mathrm{m}}{3\\,\\mathrm{s}}=2\\,\\mathrm{m/s}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>This is the <strong>gradient<\/strong> of the model line. At the\norigin the ratio\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s/t=0/0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis undefined. That does not invalidate the proportional relationship; we\nevaluate the ratio at positive times.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Original teaching graphic:<\/strong> The bilingual SVG draft\ncombines a data table, labelled axes, plotted points and a model\nline.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_005_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"A table with t=0 to 4 seconds and s=0 to 8 metres is shown as a straight line through the origin with gradient 2 m/s.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">A table with t=0 to 4 seconds and s=0 to\n8 metres is shown as a straight line through the origin with gradient 2\nm/s.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Idealised model: s = (2 m/s) · t, values are not real\nmeasurements.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example<\/h2>\n<p>At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=2.5\\,\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe model gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>2.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s=2\\,\\mathrm{m/s}\\cdot2.5\\,\\mathrm{s}=5\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAs this is between known values, it is an\n<strong>interpolation<\/strong>. A prediction of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>16<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s=16\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=8\\,\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwould be an <strong>extrapolation<\/strong> beyond the range considered.\nConstant speed would have to be checked there.<\/p>\n<p>An upward straight line is not necessarily directly proportional: it\nmust pass through\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0,0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nReal measurements may vary around a line. A graph alone does not prove\ncause and effect.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your understanding<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>s<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=3.5\\,\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin the model.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfrom\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=4\\,\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s=8\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwith a unit.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Points\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1,3)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2,5)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>7<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(3,7)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nlie on a line that misses the origin. Are the quantities directly\nproportional?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Distinguish interpolation from extrapolation.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>7<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s=7\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=8\\,\\mathrm{m}/4\\,\\mathrm{s}=2\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">No; the ratio is not constant and the line misses the origin.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Interpolation is inside, extrapolation is outside the data range\nunder consideration.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limits<\/h2>\n<p>The example assumes constant speed and suitably chosen zero points\nfor distance and time. This simple model is insufficient when the body\naccelerates or changes direction. Real measurements additionally require\nsensible scales and discussion of variation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and next steps<\/h2>\n<p>A useful graph gives axes, units and the relevant data range. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L6<\/a> examines the assumptions\nand limits of physical models.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.kmk.org/fileadmin/Dateien/veroeffentlichungen_beschluesse/2024/2024_06_13-WeBiS_Physik_MSA.pdf\">KMK\n– Educational standards in physics for the intermediate school-leaving\ncertificate (2024)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · pp. 8–11, scientific methods and inquiry · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.iqb.hu-berlin.de/media/documents/KSM_Physik.pdf\">IQB –\nPhysics proficiency-level models<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · pp. 44–48, variable control,\ngraphs and model limitations · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content\nverified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M1-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M1-L6 – Models, ranges of validity and safe experiments<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M1 – Understanding and investigating physics<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>A small object suspended from a string swings back and forth after\nbeing displaced. To describe the motion, we often draw a point on an\nideal string. This is not an exact copy of the equipment but a\ndeliberately simplified <strong>model<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From experiment to model<\/h2>\n<p>A real pendulum has a bob with physical size, a string with mass and\na pivot with friction. The <strong>simple pendulum model<\/strong> treats\nthe bob as a point mass and the string as massless and inextensible.\nFriction and air resistance are initially ignored.<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Feature<\/th>\n<th>Real system<\/th>\n<th>Simple model<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Bob<\/td>\n<td>has size and shape<\/td>\n<td>point mass<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>String<\/td>\n<td>has mass and may stretch<\/td>\n<td>massless and of fixed length<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Losses<\/td>\n<td>friction and air drag<\/td>\n<td>initially neglected<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Validity<\/td>\n<td>check for each apparatus<\/td>\n<td>especially useful for small angles and low losses<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>Length\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>l<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">l<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis measured from the pivot to the centre of mass of the bob. The\n<strong>period<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the time for a complete back-and-forth oscillation, returning to the\nsame state of motion.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Original teaching graphic:<\/strong> An SVG drawing compares\nthe real setup with an idealised model; it is not to scale. Both\nlanguage versions are included.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_006_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two pendulums show a real bob and an idealised point mass with string length l, displacement and friction indicated.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two pendulums show a real bob and an\nidealised point mass with string length l, displacement and friction\nindicated.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Real setup and simplified pendulum model have different\nassumptions; not to scale.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example<\/h2>\n<p>Suppose <strong>invented exercise readings<\/strong> give\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>20.4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">20.4\\,\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor ten full oscillations. Then:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mn>10<\/mn><\/msub><mn>10<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>20.4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><mn>10<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.04<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T=\\frac{t_{10}}{10}=\\frac{20.4\\,\\mathrm{s}}{10}=2.04\\,\\mathrm{s}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Optional extension:<\/strong> For small displacement angles,\nthe idealised approximation is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><msqrt><mrow><mi>l<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T\\approx 2\\pi\\sqrt{l/g}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIt shows the influence of string length\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>l<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">l<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand gravitational acceleration\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>g<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin the model. The approximation becomes less accurate for large angles.\nAir drag, pivot friction and real string properties may also cause\ndiscrepancies. A derivation of the formula is not required at this\nintroductory stage.<\/p>\n<p>For a safe comparison, use a low, stable arrangement, a light bob, a\nclear swing path and a small angle. Release without pushing. A teacher\nshould check the apparatus and its risks beforehand; nobody should stand\nin the swing path.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your understanding<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Name two differences between a real pendulum and the simplified\nmodel.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Twelve full oscillations take\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>24.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">24.0\\,\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFind\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is the small-angle approximation not valid for arbitrarily large\ndisplacements?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Give two safety precautions for a pendulum experiment.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">For example, a real bob has size and the string has mass; the model\nuses a point mass and massless string.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>24.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>12<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T=24.0\\,\\mathrm{s}/12=2.0\\,\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The formula relies on a small-angle approximation whose discrepancy\ngrows at larger angles.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Use a low stable support, light bob, unobstructed swing path and\nteacher supervision.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limits<\/h2>\n<p>A model is useful only when it fits the question. It can predict\nsmall-angle behaviour well but must be reassessed under other\nconditions. The drawing is not independent construction guidance; school\nexperiments must follow local safety rules and the teacher’s risk\nassessment.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and next steps<\/h2>\n<p>Models reduce complexity, make testable predictions and have limits.\nThis completes the methods module. The next module starts with <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L1 – Light sources, propagation\nand shadows<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.kmk.org/fileadmin/Dateien/veroeffentlichungen_beschluesse/2024/2024_06_13-WeBiS_Physik_MSA.pdf\">KMK\n– Educational standards in physics for the intermediate school-leaving\ncertificate (2024)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · pp. 8–11, scientific methods and inquiry · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.iqb.hu-berlin.de/media/documents/KSM_Physik.pdf\">IQB –\nPhysics proficiency-level models<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · pp. 44–48, variable control,\ngraphs and model limitations · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content\nverified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.sichere-schule.de/physik/praktisches-arbeiten/anforderungen-intro\">DGUV\n– Safe practical work in physics<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · safety, experiment instructions\nand apparatus · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.sichere-schule.de/physik/organisation-und-verantwortung/gefaehrdungsbeurteilung\">DGUV\n– Risk assessments in physics<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · teacher responsibility and\nprotective measures · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
